&#8220;I&#8217;m sorry for the men that hurt you, but I&#8217;m not sorry for the songs that came from it. Those are pretty amazing,&#8221; comedian Fortune Feimster, who co-hosts the show with Tig Notaro and Mae Martin, said at one point toward the end of the episode, adding, &#8220;Did it make you want to be a lesbian at any point?&#8221;

“I’m pansexual, so yeah,&#8221; Morissette shared in reply. &#8220;I get everything.&#8221;

GLAAD defines someone who identifies as pansexual as &#8220;a person who has the capacity to form enduring physical, romantic, and/ or emotional attractions to any person, regardless of gender identity.&#8221; The term is different than the term bisexual, which means that a person is attracted to people of more than one gender (whereas pansexual means gender is not a determining factor in attraction).

Notaro then asked, “Wait, you’re not really pansexual, are you?” to which Morissette confirmed, “I am.&#8221;

Joking that they should&#8217;ve launched the episode with this tidbit, Notaro said, &#8220;How did I not know this?&#8221;

&#8220;You know, I like to leave it for the epilogue,” quipped Morissette. “I like to confuse people on the way out. Is it confusing?”

Morissette, who&#8217;s got three Billboard 200 No. 1 albums under her belt and whose top hit &#8220;Ironic&#8221; soared to No. 4 on the Billboard Hot 100 in 1996, is married to husband Mario Treadway, aka the artist Souleye. The couple wed in 2010 and are parents to three children.

Her 14-track holiday album, Good Weather for Immaculate Conception , arrived on Friday (Nov. 6). The set was led by her recording of “Beautiful Star of Bethlehem,” featuring Brandi Carlile and Steve Martin; Morissette gets snowed-in in a music video for second single &#8220;To an Empath at Christmas Time,&#8221; released on Saturday.

&#8220;The lights are green and red and shimmering white/ These days are super dark and even quieter nights/ All this time to sense what all year felt not right/ Nothing to distract us from this heavy ride,&#8221; she sings on the opening of the midtempo &#8220;Empath.&#8221;

On the song&#8217;s chorus, Morissette says, &#8220;You don&#8217;t have to smile big for us/ Nor have a joyous time/ You won&#8217;t have to hold your breath until New Year time/ Your precious sensitivity has felt like curse and crime/ But you&#8217;re not alone with your SOS inside/ To an empath at Christmas time.&#8221;

